host tb5$ httpq https://192.168.50.1/ --verify --no-hostname-check
=== GET https://192.168.50.1/ ===
TLS: chain verified against the system store, hostname NOT checked
TRANSPORT FAILED: <urlopen error [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: unable to get local issuer certificate (_ssl.c:1108)>

  Control 2: the exported CA with the hostname check ON:
host tb5$ httpq https://192.168.50.1/ --cafile /tmp/auth1304/ck-web-ca.pem
=== GET https://192.168.50.1/ ===
TLS: chain verified against /tmp/auth1304/ck-web-ca.pem
TRANSPORT FAILED: <urlopen error [SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: IP address mismatch, certificate is not valid for '192.168.50.1'. (_ssl.c:1108)>

  Why control 2 fails.  The subject holds only a common name, CN=192.168.50.1.  A client
  that follows RFC 6125 matches an IP address only against a subjectAltName iPAddress.
  This certificate has no subjectAltName.

  The plain HTTP page at http://192.168.50.1/ still answers 200.  Its login form now
  sends to HTTPS:
<FORM action="https://192.168.50.1/index.cgi" autocomplete="off" target="_self" name="AUTH" method="POST">

  => STEP B PASS: the server now shows the ck-web certificate, not the STEP 0
     default.  The client trusts it with the exported CA.  The client does not trust it
     with the system store.  So the trust comes from the exported CA and nothing else.

